1
00:00:00,106 --> 00:00:03,098
How does a machine learning model know that it is getting better?

2
00:00:03,523 --> 00:00:04,375
It needs a score.

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:10,305
Not a feeling, not a picture, but one single number that says how wrong it is right now.

4
00:00:10,744 --> 00:00:12,234
That number is called the loss.

5
00:00:12,672 --> 00:00:18,058
Training a model means one thing: change the model so that this number gets smaller.

6
00:00:18,993 --> 00:00:21,333
Even a large language model works this way.

7
00:00:21,745 --> 00:00:29,844
In the book Build a Large Language Model, an untrained GPT model starts with a loss near eleven on its training text.

8
00:00:30,269 --> 00:00:35,628
After ten passes over the text, the training loss is below zero point four.

9
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:38,713
In this video you will build a loss by hand.

10
00:00:39,139 --> 00:00:41,160
You need the mean from video thirteen.

11
00:00:42,906 --> 00:00:44,023
Start with one guess.

12
00:00:44,448 --> 00:00:47,773
You guess that your friend is one hundred seventy centimeters tall.

13
00:00:48,198 --> 00:00:51,869
In fact, your friend is one hundred eighty centimeters tall.

14
00:00:52,294 --> 00:00:54,209
The true value is called the label, y.

15
00:00:54,648 --> 00:00:57,161
The guess is called the prediction, y hat.

16
00:00:57,613 --> 00:00:58,757
How bad was the guess?

17
00:00:59,169 --> 00:01:01,297
We want a rule that answers with one number.

18
00:01:02,239 --> 00:01:04,460
Such a rule is called a loss function.

19
00:01:04,885 --> 00:01:10,058
It takes the label and the prediction, and it returns a number that is never negative.

20
00:01:10,497 --> 00:01:12,146
Zero means a perfect guess.

21
00:01:12,585 --> 00:01:13,702
Bigger means worse.

22
00:01:14,127 --> 00:01:16,667
We write it as l of y and y hat.

23
00:01:17,106 --> 00:01:19,806
Different rules give different loss functions.

24
00:01:20,739 --> 00:01:25,806
The most common rule is the squared error: y minus y hat, all squared.

25
00:01:26,271 --> 00:01:30,287
Here, one hundred eighty minus one hundred seventy is ten.

26
00:01:30,699 --> 00:01:32,348
Ten squared is one hundred.

27
00:01:32,774 --> 00:01:34,928
So the squared error is one hundred.

28
00:01:35,354 --> 00:01:40,141
A second rule is the absolute error: the size of the gap, without its sign.

29
00:01:40,566 --> 00:01:41,510
Here it is just ten.

30
00:01:43,073 --> 00:01:45,213
The squared error grows fast.

31
00:01:45,639 --> 00:01:47,221
A gap of one costs one.

32
00:01:47,647 --> 00:01:49,362
A gap of two costs four.

33
00:01:49,775 --> 00:01:51,703
A gap of ten costs one hundred.

34
00:01:52,142 --> 00:01:56,171
The absolute error grows evenly: one, two, ten.

35
00:01:56,596 --> 00:02:00,559
So the squared error punishes big mistakes much more.

36
00:02:00,985 --> 00:02:02,807
We will see why that matters soon.

37
00:02:04,339 --> 00:02:06,174
Some tasks have no distance at all.

38
00:02:06,613 --> 00:02:08,408
Is this photo a cat or a dog?

39
00:02:08,847 --> 00:02:10,868
Here we can use the zero one loss.

40
00:02:11,307 --> 00:02:14,379
If the prediction is right, the loss is zero.

41
00:02:14,818 --> 00:02:16,985
If it is wrong, the loss is one.

42
00:02:17,411 --> 00:02:19,087
It simply counts mistakes.

43
00:02:19,525 --> 00:02:22,624
It is honest, but we will see that it is hard to learn from.

44
00:02:24,373 --> 00:02:25,516
One guess is not enough.

45
00:02:25,915 --> 00:02:30,955
A model makes many predictions, one for each example in a data set.

46
00:02:31,407 --> 00:02:34,160
Here is a toy data set with three examples.

47
00:02:34,585 --> 00:02:37,391
The labels are two, four and six.

48
00:02:37,830 --> 00:02:40,450
The model predicts one, four and eight.

49
00:02:40,889 --> 00:02:43,561
Let's compute the loss of each prediction.

50
00:02:44,506 --> 00:02:49,572
With squared error: two minus one is one, and one squared is one.

51
00:02:50,051 --> 00:02:51,767
Four minus four is zero.

52
00:02:52,205 --> 00:02:56,926
Six minus eight is minus two, and minus two squared is four.

53
00:02:57,365 --> 00:03:00,530
So the three losses are one, zero and four.

54
00:03:00,929 --> 00:03:04,306
Notice that the minus sign disappears when we square.

55
00:03:05,239 --> 00:03:11,370
Now take the mean of the losses: one plus zero plus four is five, divided by three.

56
00:03:11,808 --> 00:03:13,750
That is about one point six seven.

57
00:03:14,189 --> 00:03:17,899
This average loss over the data is called the empirical risk.

58
00:03:18,324 --> 00:03:22,074
Empirical means measured on the data we actually have.

59
00:03:23,519 --> 00:03:24,942
Here is the formula.

60
00:03:25,381 --> 00:03:32,429
R equals one over N, times the sum over n from one to N, of the loss of example n.

61
00:03:33,027 --> 00:03:35,155
N is the number of examples.

62
00:03:35,594 --> 00:03:37,482
The sum adds up all the losses.

63
00:03:37,921 --> 00:03:40,634
One over N turns the sum into a mean.

64
00:03:41,072 --> 00:03:46,618
With absolute error, the losses are one, zero and two, and the mean is one.

65
00:03:48,339 --> 00:03:50,507
Now we can use the loss to choose a model.

66
00:03:50,946 --> 00:03:52,342
Here are four toy points.

67
00:03:52,754 --> 00:03:56,185
We want a straight line through the origin that fits them.

68
00:03:56,611 --> 00:03:59,682
The line is y hat equals w times x.

69
00:04:00,108 --> 00:04:02,076
The number w is the slope.

70
00:04:02,515 --> 00:04:05,214
Different values of w give different lines.

71
00:04:06,139 --> 00:04:08,573
The number w is a parameter of the model.

72
00:04:08,985 --> 00:04:12,775
A parameter is a knob that we can turn to change what the model predicts.

73
00:04:13,174 --> 00:04:14,690
A straight line has one knob.

74
00:04:15,115 --> 00:04:19,184
A large language model has millions or billions of knobs.

75
00:04:19,623 --> 00:04:21,698
Learning means choosing the knob settings.

76
00:04:22,639 --> 00:04:24,089
Let's try some knob settings.

77
00:04:24,528 --> 00:04:27,918
With w equal to one, the line is too flat.

78
00:04:28,344 --> 00:04:31,296
The mean squared error is eight point seven five.

79
00:04:31,722 --> 00:04:38,051
With w equal to three, the line is too steep, and the loss is six point seven five.

80
00:04:38,490 --> 00:04:44,820
With w equal to two, the line is close to all points, and the loss is only zero point two five.

81
00:04:45,726 --> 00:04:48,385
Now let the computer try many values.

82
00:04:48,798 --> 00:04:54,596
On the right we draw the loss for every slope, from zero to four in small steps.

83
00:04:55,034 --> 00:04:57,029
This curve is L of w.

84
00:04:57,534 --> 00:05:01,723
Watch the line turn on the left, while a dot moves along the curve on the right.

85
00:05:02,162 --> 00:05:04,689
Each slope gives one point on the loss curve.

86
00:05:05,652 --> 00:05:09,629
The curve has the shape of a bowl, and the bottom is the best slope.

87
00:05:10,067 --> 00:05:12,408
Our demo tried four hundred and one slopes.

88
00:05:12,847 --> 00:05:19,296
The lowest loss is at w equal to two point zero seven, with a loss of about zero point two two.

89
00:05:19,722 --> 00:05:21,504
That slope is the fitted model.

90
00:05:23,206 --> 00:05:25,785
Why is the squared error so popular?

91
00:05:26,211 --> 00:05:28,206
Because its loss curve is smooth.

92
00:05:28,631 --> 00:05:32,248
At every point, the curve has a slope, a derivative.

93
00:05:32,687 --> 00:05:34,815
The slope tells us which way is downhill.

94
00:05:35,240 --> 00:05:38,299
If the slope is positive, make w smaller.

95
00:05:38,724 --> 00:05:40,865
If it is negative, make w bigger.

96
00:05:42,372 --> 00:05:44,114
Now look at the zero one loss.

97
00:05:44,553 --> 00:05:47,159
Here is a toy task with six points on a line.

98
00:05:47,598 --> 00:05:50,284
Each point has a class, zero or one.

99
00:05:50,710 --> 00:05:55,258
The rule is: predict one when x is bigger than a threshold b.

100
00:05:55,723 --> 00:05:58,024
For each b, we count the mistakes.

101
00:05:59,619 --> 00:06:01,747
The loss curve is a staircase.

102
00:06:02,185 --> 00:06:05,177
Our demo tried seven hundred and one values of b.

103
00:06:05,603 --> 00:06:08,342
It found only three different loss values.

104
00:06:08,768 --> 00:06:11,387
Between the steps the curve is completely flat.

105
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:15,776
A flat curve has slope zero, so the slope gives no direction.

106
00:06:16,215 --> 00:06:18,688
And at the steps there is no slope at all.

107
00:06:20,239 --> 00:06:25,970
That is why we usually train with a smooth loss, and we only report the count of mistakes at the end.

108
00:06:26,396 --> 00:06:30,279
The smooth loss is a stand-in that we can actually minimize.

109
00:06:30,744 --> 00:06:37,672
For classification, this stand-in is often the cross-entropy loss, a molecule we will meet later.

110
00:06:39,406 --> 00:06:42,105
The choice of loss also changes the answer.

111
00:06:42,557 --> 00:06:44,007
Here are five toy points.

112
00:06:44,445 --> 00:06:48,222
The first four lie exactly on the line y equals two x.

113
00:06:48,648 --> 00:06:52,225
The fifth point should be ten, but it was recorded as thirty.

114
00:06:52,650 --> 00:06:56,254
A point far away from the pattern like this is called an outlier.

115
00:06:57,192 --> 00:07:01,461
At w equal to two, the outlier has an error of twenty.

116
00:07:01,873 --> 00:07:04,041
The absolute error charges twenty.

117
00:07:04,466 --> 00:07:06,900
The squared error charges four hundred.

118
00:07:07,339 --> 00:07:11,926
So with squared error, this one point is louder than all the others together.

119
00:07:12,365 --> 00:07:13,655
The fit will bend toward it.

120
00:07:14,572 --> 00:07:17,298
Our demo found the best slope for each loss.

121
00:07:17,737 --> 00:07:21,447
With squared error, the best slope is three point eight two.

122
00:07:21,846 --> 00:07:24,306
The line is pulled far up by one bad point.

123
00:07:24,732 --> 00:07:28,389
With absolute error, the best slope is exactly two.

124
00:07:28,814 --> 00:07:32,272
The absolute error ignores the outlier much better.

125
00:07:33,806 --> 00:07:35,907
So neither loss is always better.

126
00:07:36,332 --> 00:07:41,731
Squared error is smooth and easy to minimize, but sensitive to outliers.

127
00:07:42,143 --> 00:07:47,383
Absolute error is robust to outliers, but it has a sharp corner at zero.

128
00:07:47,822 --> 00:07:51,465
Choosing the loss is choosing what kind of mistakes you care about.

129
00:07:53,172 --> 00:07:54,768
Now we can say what learning is.

130
00:07:55,207 --> 00:07:59,662
Learning means choosing the parameters that make the loss small on the data.

131
00:08:00,127 --> 00:08:04,023
Finding the smallest value of a function is called optimization.

132
00:08:04,449 --> 00:08:10,672
In this series, this is our last axiom, axiom E: learning equals optimization.

133
00:08:11,606 --> 00:08:14,518
Axiom E is a choice, not a theorem.

134
00:08:14,943 --> 00:08:16,858
No law of mathematics forces it.

135
00:08:17,297 --> 00:08:21,087
Machine learning simply adopts it, and it works very well.

136
00:08:21,512 --> 00:08:27,723
Every model you will meet, from a straight line to GPT, is trained by minimizing some loss.

137
00:08:29,239 --> 00:08:30,436
But how do we find the minimum?

138
00:08:30,875 --> 00:08:33,508
Trying four hundred slopes worked for one knob.

139
00:08:33,946 --> 00:08:38,069
With a billion knobs, a grid would need far too many tries.

140
00:08:38,508 --> 00:08:42,111
Instead, we follow the slope downhill, step by step.

141
00:08:42,550 --> 00:08:45,010
That method is called gradient descent.

142
00:08:45,449 --> 00:08:49,039
It is a molecule: it combines the gradient with the loss.

143
00:08:50,572 --> 00:08:54,256
And for language models, the loss is the cross-entropy.

144
00:08:54,681 --> 00:08:59,415
It uses the logarithm of the probability of the correct next token.

145
00:08:59,841 --> 00:09:04,894
Both gradient descent and cross-entropy are molecules built from this atom.

146
00:09:05,320 --> 00:09:07,035
You will find them in the molecules map.

147
00:09:08,739 --> 00:09:09,630
Now it is your turn.

148
00:09:10,082 --> 00:09:11,984
Pause the video and try this.

149
00:09:12,422 --> 00:09:14,484
The labels are three and five.

150
00:09:14,936 --> 00:09:17,077
The predictions are one and six.

151
00:09:17,502 --> 00:09:19,138
What is the mean squared error?

152
00:09:19,577 --> 00:09:21,492
And what is the mean absolute error?

153
00:09:21,917 --> 00:09:22,502
Take a moment.

154
00:09:23,452 --> 00:09:24,609
Here is the answer.

155
00:09:25,114 --> 00:09:28,678
Three minus one is two, and two squared is four.

156
00:09:29,157 --> 00:09:33,758
Five minus six is minus one, and minus one squared is one.

157
00:09:34,197 --> 00:09:39,609
The mean squared error is four plus one, divided by two, which is two point five.

158
00:09:40,048 --> 00:09:45,460
The absolute errors are two and one, so the mean absolute error is one point five.

159
00:09:47,006 --> 00:09:47,803
One more question.

160
00:09:48,242 --> 00:09:51,713
Two points: one, three, and two, six.

161
00:09:52,125 --> 00:09:55,250
The model is y hat equals w times x.

162
00:09:55,689 --> 00:10:00,224
Which slope is better, w equal to two or w equal to three?

163
00:10:00,649 --> 00:10:02,365
Use the mean squared error.

164
00:10:02,790 --> 00:10:03,468
Pause and try.

165
00:10:04,406 --> 00:10:08,368
With w equal to two, the predictions are two and four.

166
00:10:08,807 --> 00:10:14,445
The errors are one and two, so the squared errors are one and four, and the mean is two point five.

167
00:10:14,871 --> 00:10:19,379
With w equal to three, the predictions are three and six.

168
00:10:19,791 --> 00:10:22,557
Both are exact, so the loss is zero.

169
00:10:22,996 --> 00:10:24,592
The slope three wins.

170
00:10:26,306 --> 00:10:28,925
Let's check everything in Python with NumPy.

171
00:10:29,417 --> 00:10:33,978
First we compute squared and absolute errors for the three predictions.

172
00:10:34,404 --> 00:10:38,819
Then we try four hundred and one slopes and keep the one with the smallest loss.

173
00:10:39,258 --> 00:10:41,731
Then we count mistakes with the zero one loss.

174
00:10:42,197 --> 00:10:46,651
Finally, we fit the data with the outlier, using both losses.

175
00:10:48,206 --> 00:10:52,195
This output is real; the script ran while the video was made.

176
00:10:52,647 --> 00:10:57,900
The mean squared error is one point six seven, and the mean absolute error is one.

177
00:10:58,325 --> 00:11:00,878
The best slope is two point zero seven.

178
00:11:01,317 --> 00:11:04,788
The zero one loss takes only three different values.

179
00:11:05,227 --> 00:11:11,011
And with the outlier, squared error gives three point eight two, while absolute error gives two.

180
00:11:12,739 --> 00:11:13,377
In summary.

181
00:11:13,789 --> 00:11:20,053
A loss function turns how wrong one prediction is into one number that is never negative.

182
00:11:20,478 --> 00:11:25,890
Squared error, absolute error and the zero one loss are three common choices.

183
00:11:26,329 --> 00:11:29,401
The mean loss over a data set is the empirical risk.

184
00:11:29,827 --> 00:11:32,034
Parameters are the knobs of the model.

185
00:11:32,939 --> 00:11:37,460
Learning is optimization: choose the parameters that make the loss small.

186
00:11:37,886 --> 00:11:43,577
Smooth losses are easier to minimize, and squared error punishes outliers hard.

187
00:11:44,003 --> 00:11:50,984
Next, in video sixteen, the last atom: the activation function, the bend that makes networks deep.

188
00:11:51,410 --> 00:11:51,955
See you there.

189
00:11:53,872 --> 00:11:55,694
This video was made by David R.

190
00:11:56,133 --> 00:11:59,364
You can find more lessons at David Ramirez J dot com.

191
00:11:59,923 --> 00:12:00,641
Thanks for watching.
